# DB.nomics

DB.nomics is a platform that aggregates open access economic data (and
much more: energy data, population data...) from many sources:
<https://db.nomics.world/>

We will use a R package called rdbnomics, that allows us to download
data by identifier directly from your R code. A good alternative is
pdfetch (<https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/pdfetch/index.html>).
Alternatives exist, such as the download.file function; the more
ambitious ones can also crawl a website to scrape together an original
data set...

In this report, I will download yearly data on French real GDP (base
year 2010), as provided by Eurostat, the European statistical agency,
and real GDP at 2020 reference levels from AMECO.[^1] AMECO is the
annual macro-economic database of the European Commission's Directorate
General for Economic and Financial Affairs. Quoting its frontpage on the
web
(<https://economy-finance.ec.europa.eu/economic-research-and-databases/economic-databases/ameco-database_en>):
"The database contains data for the EU, the euro area, EU Member States,
candidate countries and other OECD countries, for a total of over 40
countries. Data is organized in eighteen chapters, from population and
employment via main national accounts aggregates such as gross domestic
product to sector accounts and the cyclical adjustment of public finance
variables."

[^1]: Of course, the series are not identical, since in what case 2010
    prices are used, and in the other case 2020 price levels are used,
    to build GDP in volumes.

# Time-series related packages in R

This page contains lots of references to R packages that are related to
time series: <https://cran.r-project.org/web/views/TimeSeries.html>. The
page is curated by the authors of some of these packages, that strive to
keep it up to date. A great feature is that this page regroups packages
based on what one may want to do. It is a great way to start if you have
no idea how to implement a given class of tests or models!

In what follows, I will load several of these packages. Note that some
packages introduce the same functions, and sometimes they are just
wrap-arounds of the stats package from the base distribution. In your
project, you are advised to stick to a small number of packages to avoid
clashes: if you don't, you run a risk that two packages will try to
overwrite the same function from the base distribution, that these
versions will not be compatible to other functions or methods, resulting
in errors that you will have a hard time to debug. To clarify my method
here, the aim of introducing different packages is to give you an
overview of what is available, and to identify some tools that might be
useful to you.

You can also check the more general page on econometrics, which can be
useful for any kind of applied econometrics project (not only time
series): <https://cran.r-project.org/web/views/Econometrics.html>

Alongside packages, these pages contain data sets that you may want to
explore. Keep in mind that these data sets are not very original, and
that you had better find (or build) the data set that best suits the
economic question you are asking yourselves. **Start your project from
the economic question, not from the data set!**

# Downloading French GDP data

```{r Load GDP from DBnomics }
library(rdbnomics)
library(tidyverse)

# fpp3 library, unused for this report
# library(fpp3)

# Time disaggregation library, unused 
# library(td)

# Data source from the AMECO database, French real GDP at 2020 reference levels
gdp_AMECO <- rdb(ids="AMECO/OVGD/FRA.1.1.0.0.OVGD")

# Alternative data source from Eurostat, real French GDP at 2010 reference levels
gdp <- rdb(ids="Eurostat/nama_10_gdp/A.CLV10_MEUR.B1GQ.FR")
```

The R code chunk above downloads real GDP at 2010 reference levels from
Eurostat, as well as real GDP at 2020 reference levels from AMECO. This
allows me to introduce some standardized macro time series identifiers
(example "Eurostat/nama_10_gdp/A.CLV10_MEUR.B1GQ.FR") :

- A means annual
- CLV10 means "chain linked volumes, base 2010"
- MEUR : million euro
- B1GQ : code in national accounts nomenclature for GDP evaluated at
  market prices
- FR is for France

Here is a screenshot of the data selection page, where I have applied
successive filters to obtain the time series from AMECO:
![Image](screenshot-PIB_France-AMECO-dbnomics.png) By browsing on
DB.nomics webpages, you can find the macro time series you want, then
copy-paste its identifier to download the data directly from your R
code. This is an essential step for reproducibility!

# Graphical representations and descriptive statistics

Let us first represent our series in level, its logarithm and its growth
rate (approximated by the first difference of its log). Looking at the
log, one can spot the structural break in the data: the French economy
will never be the same after the mid 70s. From the point of view of
French economic history, this marks the end of the "Trente glorieuses"
(1945-1975). France is far from unique here: at the international level,
the aftermath of the first oil shock (1973) and second oil shock (1979)
is characterized by persistent, high inflation, high unemployment and
sluggish growth (stagflation).

```{r Graphs GDP}
library(ggplot2)

# Get a tidy dataframe for GDP values called gdp2 
# The name is gdp2 for no specific reason other than there used to be a gdp1 series in my code :)
gdp2 <- data.frame(period = gdp_AMECO$period, value = gdp_AMECO$value)

# Column for the log of GDP
gdp2$log_value <- log(gdp2$value)

# Column for the growth rate of GDP in %, as approximated by Delta ln Y
# Careful: the series in difference does not exist for 1960, since we don't have the value for 1959
# We are adding a NA value in 1960, so that all the series fit together in the same dataframe
gdp2$growth_rate <- c(NA, diff(gdp2$log_value) * 100)

# Plot GDP in levels
ggplot(gdp2, aes(x = period, y = value)) +
  geom_line(color = "blue") +
  labs(title = "French GDP in levels (base year 2020))", x = "Year", y = "Million euros")

# Plot GDP in log
ggplot(gdp2, aes(x = period, y = log_value)) +
  geom_line(color = "red") +
  labs(title = "Log of GDP", x = "Year", y = "log(PIB)")

# Plot annual growth rate in percentage points
ggplot(gdp2, aes(x = period, y = growth_rate)) +
  geom_line(color = "black") +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, color = "black", linetype = "dashed", linewidth = 0.5) + 
# Line above adds a horizontal line at 0% growth rate, as a visual aid
  labs(title = "Annual growth rate, French GDP", x = "Year", y = "%")
```

Let us get some descriptive statistics about the growth rate of French
GDP, as well as a density plot and three additional standardized moments
(variance, skewness and kurtosis):

```{r Descriptive stats for the growth rate}
library(TSA)
library(astsa)

# Descriptive statistics
summary(gdp2$growth_rate, na.rm=TRUE)

#Graph the density
ggplot(gdp2) +
  aes(x = growth_rate) +
  geom_density()

# Moments
library(moments)
var(gdp2$growth_rate,na.rm=TRUE)
skewness(gdp2$growth_rate, na.rm=TRUE)
kurtosis(gdp2$growth_rate, na.rm=TRUE)
```

Here I'm computing or graphing many different things, that are not super
useful in this study so I won't comment them. But as this section
corresponds to exploratory data analysis, I leave here some functions
that might be useful depending on your use case. This may sound trivial,
but **in exploratory data analysis, you have to explore!** Sometimes you
will find something troubling or revealing in the data that will be
worth investigating, even though it didn't originally correspond to your
research question. Keep your eyes open!

# Stationarity tests

Next we study the stationarity of the logarithm of GDP. The
autocorrelogram alone suggests long time persistence of the series, and
warrants a stationarity test.

```{r Stationarity of log}
library(astsa)
aux <- acf2(gdp2$log_value, main = "Autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation of log(Y)")

library(tseries)
library(urca)
test.df <- ur.df(gdp2$log_value, lags=2)
summary(test.df)
```

Here we use the Augmented Dickey-Fuller test. The null hypothesis of a
unit root cannot be rejected. The series in log is not stationary. Of
course, we already knew that GDP has a trend, so that its time series
could not possibly be stationary. The decomposition between trend and
cycle is something that we don't tackle here: we are doing a pure
forecasting exercise, without any causal interpretation. If your
research project involves the study of the business cycle, you will not
escape spending time on the trend-cycle decomposition...

# Model specification

Here, we will go ahead and try to get a model for the growth rate of GDP
($\Delta \ln Y_t \approx g_{Y,t}$).

Let us now get the autocorrelograms of the growth rate of GDP. We also
test the growth rate for stationarity.

```{r Stationarity of the growth rate}
test.df.gr <- ur.df(gdp2$growth_rate[-1], lags=2)
summary(test.df.gr)

library(astsa)
aux2 <- acf2(gdp2$growth_rate, main = "Autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation of GDP growth rate")
```

The ADF test rejects the null hypothesis that $g_Y$ has a unit root at
the 5% significance level, but not at the 1% significance level.
Remember that the small sample size means this type of tests are
unreliable anyway.

Here, the autocorrelation decays slowly as we move up the orders, while
the partial autocorrelogram suggests the first order is highly
significant, and the second and third order almost so. This will suggest
specifying the model as an autoregressive model, with orders between 1
and 3. Recall the general formulation of an AR(p) process:
$$y_t = c + \rho_1 y_{t-1} + \rho_2 y_{t-2} + \dots \rho_1 y_{t-p} +\epsilon_t  $$

# Estimation and diagnostics

## Estimating an AR(2) model

Let us try to estimate an AR(2) model for the growth rate of GDP. A more
complete analysis would require you to also estimate an AR(1) and an
AR(3), of course!

```{r Build an xts time series object from the growth rate of GDP, then fit an AR(2) model}
library(xts)
xts_gdp <- xts(gdp2$growth_rate, order.by = gdp2$period)

# Here we get rid of the 1st line, which is NA, and the last one which is a forecast for 2026 (not actual observation data)
xts_gdp <- xts_gdp[-c(1,nrow(xts_gdp)),]
#tail(xts_gdp)
#head(xts_gdp)

library(forecast)
#xts_gdp %>%
#  Arima(order=c(2,0,0)) %>%
#  forecast(h=3) %>%
#  autoplot
ar2_gdp <- xts_gdp %>%
  Arima(order=c(2,0,0))
summary(ar2_gdp)

# Check whether the inverse of AR roots are within the unit circle (additional check for stationarity)
#autoplot(ar2_gdp)

# Other way of presenting some results of the regression
# library(pander)
# pander(ar2_gdp,style="rmarkdown")

# Add the residuals and fitted values of the AR(2) model to the original dataset
res_ar2 <- residuals(ar2_gdp)
fitted_ar2 <- fitted(ar2_gdp)

# Extract the values from the xts object
resid_values <- as.numeric(res_ar2)
fitted_values <- as.numeric(fitted_ar2)

# Create vectors with NA in the first and last positions
col_resid <- c(NA, resid_values, NA)
col_fitted <- c(NA, fitted_values, NA)

# Add the new vectors as columns to gdp2
gdp2$resid_ar2_gr <- col_resid
gdp2$fitted_ar2_gr <- col_fitted
#head(gdp2)


# Plot actual growth rate and fitted values
ggplot(gdp2, aes(x = period)) +
  geom_line(aes(y = growth_rate, color = "Actual Growth Rate"), linewidth = 1) +
  geom_line(aes(y = fitted_ar2_gr, color = "Fitted AR(2) Growth Rate"), linewidth = 1) +
  labs(
    title = "Comparison of Actual and Fitted Growth Rates",
    x = "Period",
    y = "Growth Rate",
    color = "Legend"
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Actual Growth Rate" = "blue", "Fitted AR(2) Growth Rate" = "red")) +
  theme_minimal()

```

As can be expected, the fitted AR(2) model makes large mistakes whenever
the growth rate varies unexpectedly.

## Model diagnostics

Let us perform some checks on the residuals. The firs step is to plot
them.

```{r Model diagnostics for the AR(2)}

# Plot actual growth rate and fitted values
ggplot(gdp2, aes(x = period)) +
  geom_point(aes(y = resid_ar2_gr, color = "Residuals")) +
  labs(
    title = "Residuals of the AR(2) estimation",
    x = "Period",
    y = "Residuals",
    color = "Legend"
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Residuals" = "blue")) +
  theme_minimal()

# The following code used to look at a subset of the data
subset_gdp2 <- gdp2 %>% mutate(year = year(period)) %>%  # Extract the year from the date
  filter(year >= 1970 & year <= 1980)
```

Looking at the residuals, it appears that there are 4 clear outliers
(1975, 2009, and of course 2020 and 2021). Residuals also look
autocorrelated (in particular they do not alternate between positive and
negative values as you would expect from a white noise). Determining
whether they are normal or homoskedastic with eyes only is really hard,
especially for such a small sample.

Ideally, we would like to be confident in the fact that the residuals
are pure innovations, that is are white noise. To do so, let us perform
tests for absence of autocorrelation, normality, heteroskedasticity.

```{r Test on the residuals for the AR(2)}

library(FinTS)

#Ljung-Box test for autocorrelation
ljung_box_result <- Box.test(gdp2$resid_ar2, type = "Ljung-Box", lag = 10, fitdf=2)
print(ljung_box_result)

auxx <- acf2(gdp2$resid_ar2_gr, main = "Autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation of the residuals")

# Shapiro-Wilk test for normality, better than Jarque-Bera on small sample
shapiro_result <- shapiro.test(gdp2$resid_ar2)
print(shapiro_result)

# Tests for heteroskedasticity

# Test for autocorrelation in the squares of the residuals
ljung_box_squares_residuals_arch <- Box.test(gdp2$resid_ar2_gr^2,lag=5,type="Ljung-Box",fitdf=2)
print(ljung_box_squares_residuals_arch)

# LM test for heteroskedasticity
arch_result <- ArchTest(gdp2$resid_ar2)
print(arch_result)


```

The Ljung-Box test fails to reject the null hypothesis of no
autocorrelation up to order 10. This is confirmed by plotting the
autocorrelogram and partial autocorrelogram of the residuals.

The Shapiro-Wilk test strongly rejects the null hypothesis of normality.

Concerning the tests of heteroskedasticity: the Ljung-Box test on the
squares of residuals rejects at the 10% significance level the null
hypothesis that there is no autocorrelation on the squares of residuals,
which is inconclusive. The ARCH LM-test rejects the absence of
heteroskedasticity.

Here is a more concise command to graphically check residuals

```{r Other checks for residuals}
checkresiduals(ar2_gdp)

